From 6a4a988fad7dcd046635afd9f28766c635703ccd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Neivin Detlefsen <neivin.detlefsen@gmx.de>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 18:30:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] fix: set i and clear d on interrupt entry, report
 dispatch misses

The frame drivers pushed the interrupt frame and ran the handler with the
interrupted code's I and D flags. The 65816 sets I and clears D after the
push (interp816 doInterrupt does the same), so a handler ran with IRQs
unmasked and, after a SED in the interrupted code, in decimal mode. In
FF6 the co-sim showed it as stack residue: the php of CutsceneNMI pushed
P = $A1 where the reference pushed $A5. cpu_enter_interrupt_flags() now
follows every push in the beam and the generic frame driver (NMI, IRQ,
and the raster IRQ of the generic draw walk).

A dispatch value missing from a generated switch reaches the default
arm's cpu_trace_dispatch_oob call. Without SNESRECOMP_TRACE that call was
an empty inline, and for a call-form dispatch without a live pointer the
arm then unpops the call frame and falls through: the handler is skipped
without a miss, a tripwire or a log line. FF6's wound handler ($C24600,
missing from jsr (SetStatusTbl,x) at $C243AC) went unnoticed that way.
The call now lands in an always-on per-site table in common_cpu_infra.c,
like the unresolved-abandon accounting: one "[dispatch-oob]" stderr line
on the first hit per site and a dispatch_oob section in the post-mortem
JSON. The trace build records the same and keeps its slot capture.

The trace build's debug server keeps its frame history in static
storage, about 200 KB per frame. At 6000 frames that is 1.2 GB, and with
the trace rings a Windows trace build had a 2.23 GB image and did not
load. The size is now SNESRECOMP_FRAME_HISTORY_SIZE (default 6000), set
from the CMake cache variable of the same name in runner.cmake.

Tests that fail on the old state: beam_frame_driver_test and the new
generic_frame_driver_test check the handler's I, D and P and the pushed P
for NMI and IRQ; dispatch_oob_test checks the stderr lines and the JSON
table of a production build; frame_history_size_test compiles
debug_server.c at the default and at 16 frames and requires the static
data to shrink by at least the WRAM and VRAM copies of the dropped
frames. All four run in run_c_tests.sh.
